   "source": [
    "### Provided example — Estimate distance with a known position"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "source": [
    "We generate observations with $x_0=20$ and $y_0=15$, then estimate $y_0$ while treating $x_0$ as known.\n",
    "\n",
    "The following worked example provides the density from Exercise 1 and the code to use it. Run it after attempting your derivation."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "source": [
    "Then, we wish to find\n",
    "\n",
    "$$p(y_0 | \\{x_k\\}, x_0)$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Using Bayes’ theorem:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$p(y_0 | \\{x_k\\}, x_0) = \\frac{p(y_0 , \\{x_k\\}, x_0)}{p(\\{x_k\\}, x_0)} = \\frac{p(\\{x_k\\} | y_0, x_0)p(y_0 | x_0)p(x_0)}{p(\\{x_k\\} | x_0)p(x_0)}\\propto p(\\{x_k\\} | y_0, x_0) p(y_0)$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where the last step uses the prior assumption that  $y_0 \\perp x_0$, and so we have $p(y_0 | x_0) = p(y_0)$.\n",
    "The most intuitive prior $p(y_0)$ when we don't have additional information is the one which is uniform over a large enough segment $[y_{min}, y_{max}]$, i.e. $p(y_0) = 1/(y_{max}-y_{min})$\n",
    "\n",
    "Even if this seems to be a good choice in general, we will compare it with another prior below.\n",
    "For the data likelihood $p(\\{x_k\\} | y_0, x_0)$ we consider that the observations $x_k$ are independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.), so:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$p(\\{x_k\\} | y_0, x_0) = \\prod_{k=1}^N p(x_k | y_0, x_0)$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Within the uniform prior's support, we get:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$p(y_0 | \\{x_k\\}, x_0) \\propto \\prod_{k=1}^N p(x_k | y_0, x_0)$$\n",
    "\n",
    "If the likelihood maximum lies within that support, maximizing the posterior coincides with maximizing the log-likelihood $L(\\{x_k\\} | x_0,y_0)$"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "source": [
    "We generate $N=100$ flashes and plot the log-likelihood as a function of $y_0$."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 15,
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "from scipy.stats import cauchy\n",
    "from scipy.optimize import minimize\n",
    "\n",
    "np.random.seed(123456)  # Reproduce the simulations when running all cells in order."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "source": [
    "Define `loglh` to compute the log-likelihood for observations `x`, given the position `x0` and positive distance `y0`."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 16,
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def loglh(x0, y0, x):\n",
    "    if y0 <= 0:\n",
    "        return -np.inf\n",
    "    return np.sum(np.log(y0) - np.log(np.pi) - np.log(y0**2 + (x - x0)**2))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "source": [
    "Generate observations from the Cauchy distribution using `scipy.stats.cauchy`. Its location is $x_0$ and its scale is $y_0$."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 17,
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "N = 100\n",
    "x0_true = 20\n",
    "y0_true = 15\n",
    "\n",
    "x = cauchy.rvs(loc=x0_true, scale=y0_true, size=N)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "source": [
    "Compute the log-likelihood for a range of positive distances around the true value $y_0^*$ and plot it."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 18,
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 720x480 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "tab_y0 = np.linspace(0, 2 * y0_true, 200)[1:]\n",
    "logl_y0 = np.array([loglh(x0_true, y0, x) for y0 in tab_y0])\n",
    "\n",
    "fig = plt.figure(figsize=(9, 6), dpi=80)\n",
    "plt.title('Log-likelihood', fontsize=16)\n",
    "plt.xlabel(r'$y_0$', fontsize=14)\n",
    "plt.ylabel(r'$L(y_0)$', fontsize=14)\n",
    "plt.plot(tab_y0, logl_y0)\n",
    "plt.axvline(y0_true, color='r', label=r'True $y_0$')\n",
    "plt.legend(fontsize=14);"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "source": [
    "To display the same curve on a relative-likelihood scale, plot $\\exp(L-L_{\\max})$. Subtracting the maximum keeps the values numerically manageable.\n",
    "\n",
    "Under a uniform prior, this has the shape of the posterior within the prior's support. Its maximum is one; it is not a normalized probability density."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 19,
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 720x480 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "fig = plt.figure(figsize=(9, 6), dpi=80)\n",
    "plt.title('Relative likelihood', fontsize=16)\n",
    "plt.xlabel(r'$y_0$', fontsize=14)\n",
    "plt.ylabel(r'$e^{L-L_{\\max}}$', fontsize=14)\n",
    "plt.plot(tab_y0, np.exp(logl_y0 - logl_y0.max()))\n",
    "plt.axvline(y0_true, color='r', label=r'True $y_0$')\n",
    "plt.legend(fontsize=14);"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "source": [
    "Next, compute the maximum likelihood estimate $\\widehat{y}_{0,ML}$ for several sample sizes and compare it with the true distance."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "source": [
    "Use `scipy.optimize.minimize` to minimize the negative log-likelihood. To keep the distance positive, optimize `log_y0` and recover `y0 = np.exp(log_y0)`. We still evaluate the same likelihood at that distance.\n",
    "\n",
    "The provided `checked_minimize` helper checks that the optimizer has converged before its result is used."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 20,
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "CPU times: user 10 ms, sys: 1.94 ms, total: 12 ms\n",
      "Wall time: 10.6 ms\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "%%time\n",
    "\n",
    "def checked_minimize(objective, initial_guess, bounds=None):\n",
    "    result = minimize(objective, initial_guess, method='Nelder-Mead', bounds=bounds)\n",
    "    if not result.success or not np.isfinite(result.fun) or not np.all(np.isfinite(result.x)):\n",
    "        raise RuntimeError(f'Optimization failed: {result.message}')\n",
    "    return result.x\n",
    "\n",
    "tab_N = np.unique(np.logspace(0, 4, num=10).astype(int))\n",
    "y0_ML = []\n",
    "for N in tab_N:\n",
    "    x = cauchy.rvs(loc=x0_true, scale=y0_true, size=N)\n",
    "    y0_guess = np.median(np.abs(x - x0_true))\n",
    "    log_y0 = checked_minimize(\n",
    "        lambda params: -loglh(x0_true, np.exp(params[0]), x),\n",
    "        [np.log(y0_guess)],\n",
    "    )[0]\n",
    "    y0_ML.append(np.exp(log_y0))"
   ]
  },
