   },
   "source": [
    "**Your answer:** Compare the sample mean and median as position estimators.\n",
    "\n",
    "The sample median converges to x0 = 20 as N increases and stays stable, unlike\n",
    "the sample mean, which never settles. This is because the Cauchy distribution has no\n",
    "finite mean, so the mean is not a consistent estimator, while the median (unaffected by\n",
    "extreme values) remains a valid, consistent one."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "source": [
    "Next we use mean squared error to compare the median with maximum likelihood estimation."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "source": [
    "## Part 2 — Comparing estimators"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "source": [
    "**Mean squared error (MSE)** is the expected squared difference between an estimator and the true parameter:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\mathrm{MSE}(\\widehat{\\lambda};\\lambda)=\\mathbb{E}_{\\lambda}\\left[(\\widehat{\\lambda}-\\lambda)^2\\right].$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The expectation is over repeated datasets generated with the same true parameter $\\lambda$. We approximate it by repeating the simulation."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "source": [
    "### Recall — Fisher information"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "source": [
    "As we recalled earlier, for $N$ independent events we have\n",
    "$$\n",
    "I_N(\\lambda)=\\sum_{i=1}^N I(\\lambda) = NI(\\lambda).\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Therefore for our problem\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "I_N(x_0, y_0) = -N \\mathbb{E}_x\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\partial^2 L}{\\partial x_0^2} & \\frac{\\partial^2 L}{\\partial x_0\\partial y_0}\\\\ \\frac{\\partial^2 L}{\\partial y_0\\partial x_0} & \\frac{\\partial^2 L}{\\partial y_0^2}\\end{bmatrix}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "And after a bit of computations, and a little help from [Wolfram Alpha](https://www.wolframalpha.com/calculators/integral-calculator/), one finds that if $x \\in(-\\infty,+\\infty)$ then the Fisher information is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$I_N(x_0, y_0) =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{N}{2y_0^2} & 0\\\\0 & \\frac{N}{2y_0^2}\\end{bmatrix} = \\frac{N}{2y_0^2} \\mathbb{I}_2$$\n",
    "\n",
    "And therefore\n",
    "\n",
    "*   $\\det [I_N(x_0,y_0)] = \\frac{N^2}{4y_0^4}$\n",
    "*   $I_N(y_0|x_0) = \\frac{N}{2y_0^2}$\n",
    "*   $I_N(x_0|y_0) = \\frac{N}{2y_0^2} = \\text{constant in }x_0$"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "source": [
    "### Recall — Cramér–Rao bound"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "source": [
    "For an **unbiased estimator**, the Cramér–Rao bound gives\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\mathrm{MSE}(\\widehat{\\lambda}_i;\\lambda_i)\\geq\\{[I_N(\\boldsymbol{\\lambda})]^{-1}\\}_{ii}.$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Maximum likelihood and maximum a posteriori estimators need not be unbiased for small samples. We use this bound as a reference when comparing them; a biased estimator can have MSE below the unbiased-estimator bound."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "source": [
    "### Recall — Jeffreys prior"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "source": [
    "In general, the Jeffreys prior is given by\n",
    "\n",
    "$$p(\\mathbf{\\lambda}) \\propto \\sqrt{\\det[I_N(\\mathbf{\\lambda})]}$$"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "source": [
    "### Provided example — Compare distance estimates"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "source": [
    "Treat $x_0=20$ as known and estimate $y_0$. For an unbiased estimator of the distance, the Cramér–Rao bound is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\mathrm{MSE}(\\widehat{y}_0;y_0)\\geq\\frac{2y_0^2}{N}.$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The one-parameter Jeffreys prior is $p(y_0)\\propto1/y_0$. Thus\n",
    "\n",
    "$$p(y_0\\mid\\{x_k\\},x_0)\\propto\\frac{1}{y_0}\\prod_{k=1}^N\\frac{1}{\\pi}\\frac{y_0}{(x_k-x_0)^2+y_0^2}.$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Maximizing this posterior gives the **maximum a posteriori (MAP)** estimate. We only need the posterior up to proportionality for this calculation."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "source": [
    "Compute the MSE of the ML and MAP distance estimators and compare them with the Cramér–Rao reference."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "source": [
    "First, define `jeffpost`, the log-posterior up to an additive constant for the prior proportional to `1 / y0`."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 31,
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def jeffpost(x0, y0, x):\n",
    "    if y0 <= 0:\n",
    "        return -np.inf\n",
    "    return loglh(x0, y0, x) - np.log(y0)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "source": [
    "The provided `minimizer_y0` helper simulates repeated datasets and estimates the distance. Its arguments specify the known position, sample size, and number of repetitions."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 32,
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def minimizer_y0(func, y0, x0, N, N_rep):\n",
    "    y0_est = np.zeros(N_rep)\n",
    "    for ir in range(N_rep):\n",
    "        x = cauchy.rvs(loc=x0, scale=y0, size=N)\n",
    "        y0_guess = np.median(np.abs(x - x0))\n",
    "        log_y0 = checked_minimize(\n",
    "            lambda params: -func(x0, np.exp(params[0]), x),\n",
    "            [np.log(y0_guess)],\n",
    "        )[0]\n",
    "        y0_est[ir] = np.exp(log_y0)\n",
    "    return y0_est"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "source": [
    "To estimate MSE, repeat each fit $N_{rep}=500$ times, with $N=10$ flashes per dataset, for $N_{est}=15$ true distances in $(0,1]$."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 33,
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "N_est=15\n",
    "tab_y0=np.linspace(0,1,N_est+1)[1:]\n",
    "N = 10\n",
    "N_rep = 500"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 34,
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "CPU times: user 5.75 s, sys: 15.6 ms, total: 5.76 s\n",
      "Wall time: 5.85 s\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "%%time\n",
    "\n",
    "y0_ML = np.array([minimizer_y0(loglh, y0, x0_true, N, N_rep) for y0 in tab_y0])\n",
    "y0_J = np.array([minimizer_y0(jeffpost, y0, x0_true, N, N_rep) for y0 in tab_y0])"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "source": [
    "For each true distance, average the squared errors over the repeated fits. Bootstrap these squared errors to describe uncertainty from the finite number of simulation repetitions."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "source": [
    "The provided `MSE_est` helper returns the empirical MSE and the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles of its bootstrap estimates. These intervals concern the simulated MSE; the final exercise instead bootstraps the observed flashes to obtain intervals for the fitted parameters."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 35,
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def MSE_est(estimates, true_value, N_boot=100):\n",
    "    squared_errors = (np.asarray(estimates) - true_value)**2\n",
    "    mse = squared_errors.mean()\n",
    "    bootstrap_errors = np.random.choice(\n",
    "        squared_errors, size=(N_boot, len(squared_errors)), replace=True\n",
    "    )\n",
    "    bootstrap_means = bootstrap_errors.mean(axis=1)\n",
    "    ci_min, ci_max = np.percentile(bootstrap_means, [2.5, 97.5])\n",
    "    return mse, ci_min, ci_max"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "source": [
    "Compute the empirical MSE and bootstrap interval for each true distance."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 36,
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "MSE_ML_10 = np.zeros(N_est)\n",
    "CI_min_ML_10 = np.zeros(N_est)\n",
    "CI_max_ML_10 = np.zeros(N_est)\n",
    "MSE_J_10 = np.zeros(N_est)\n",
    "CI_min_J_10 = np.zeros(N_est)\n",
    "CI_max_J_10 = np.zeros(N_est)\n",
    "\n",
    "for j,y0 in enumerate(tab_y0):\n",
    "  MSE, CI_min, CI_max = MSE_est(y0_ML[j], y0)\n",
    "  MSE_ML_10[j] = MSE\n",
    "  CI_min_ML_10[j] = CI_min\n",
    "  CI_max_ML_10[j] = CI_max\n",
    "  MSE, CI_min, CI_max = MSE_est(y0_J[j], y0)\n",
    "  MSE_J_10[j] = MSE\n",
    "  CI_min_J_10[j] = CI_min\n",
    "  CI_max_J_10[j] = CI_max"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "source": [
    "Plot the empirical MSEs and bootstrap intervals alongside the Cramér–Rao reference."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 37,
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 720x480 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "def plot_mse_interval(parameters, mse, ci_min, ci_max, color, label):\n",
    "    plt.plot(parameters, mse, color=color, label=label)\n",
    "    plt.vlines(parameters, ci_min, ci_max, color=color, alpha=0.6)\n",
    "\n",
    "fig = plt.figure(figsize=(9, 6), dpi=80)\n",
    "plt.title(f'Scale estimation, N={N}', fontsize=16)\n",
    "plt.xlabel(r'$y_0$', fontsize=14)\n",
    "plt.ylabel('Empirical MSE', fontsize=14)\n",
    "plot_mse_interval(tab_y0, MSE_J_10, CI_min_J_10, CI_max_J_10, 'g', 'MAP')\n",
    "plot_mse_interval(tab_y0, MSE_ML_10, CI_min_ML_10, CI_max_ML_10, 'r', 'ML')\n",
    "plt.plot(tab_y0, 2 * tab_y0**2 / N, label='Cramér–Rao reference')\n",
    "plt.legend(fontsize=14);"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "provided"
    ]
   },
   "source": [
