   "source": [
    "**Your answer:** Interpret the ML/MAP comparison across sample sizes.\n",
    "\n",
    "At N=3, the ML estimator has a much larger MSE than the MAP estimator across\n",
    "almost the entire range of y0, with wide bootstrap intervals reflecting high variability from\n",
    "only 1,000 repetitions with so little data. The MAP curve tracks the Cramér–Rao reference\n",
    "closely, while ML overshoots it substantially, especially at large y0. \n",
    "\n",
    "At N=10, the gap between ML and MAP narrows considerably, and both curves lie closer to the reference, though\n",
    "ML still sits noticeably above MAP, especially as y0 increases. \n",
    "\n",
    "At N=50, ML and MAP are closer and both hug the Cramér–Rao reference tightly, showing that the prior's influence becomes negligible as the likelihood dominates. \n",
    "\n",
    "The MAP estimator remains relatively stable across sample sizes, while the ML estimator exhibits substantial variability for small \\(N\\). As the sample size increases, the ML estimator becomes more stable and progressively approaches the MAP estimate. This illustrates the regularizing effect of the prior in the MAP estimator, which is particularly relevant when only a small amount of data is available."
   ]
