   "source": [
    "### Exercise 5 — Compare position estimates\n",
    "\n",
    "Treat $y_0=15$ as known and consider true positions $x_0\\in[-1,1]$.\n",
    "\n",
    "1. What does the one-parameter Jeffreys prior give for $x_0$? What does this imply for ML and MAP position estimates?\n",
    "2. Start with $N=10$ and 500 repetitions. Compare the empirical MSE of the ML and sample-median estimators across the true positions, and plot their bootstrap intervals and the Cramér–Rao reference.\n",
    "3. Repeat for $N=3$ with 1,000 repetitions and $N=50$ with 200 repetitions. Plot all three sample sizes together and interpret the comparison."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "exercise"
    ]
   },
   "source": [
    "**Your answer:** Explain the Jeffreys prior for position when the distance is known.\n",
    "\n",
    "For a location family such as the Cauchy distribution with y0 fixed, the\n",
    "Fisher information $I(x0) = 1/(2y0^2)$ does not depend on x0 itself (it is the same everywhere\n",
    "on the line, since shifting x0 just translates the whole density). The Jeffreys prior, being\n",
    "proportional to $sqrt(I(x0))$, is therefore a constant, i.e. a flat (uniform) prior on x0. This\n",
    "means that, up to the choice of prior support, the MAP estimate of x0 coincides exactly with\n",
    "the ML estimate : adding a flat prior does not change the location of the posterior mode\n",
    "relative to the likelihood maximum."
   ]
  },
